Z||000000||BroCar 87||01-31-2001||09:35 PM||||I have been reading through some old posts about the V8 Fiero, and have noticed that you said (quite often) your front end feels light up in the mph's. Have you remedy'd that? I was thinking. Maybe if we just use lowering springs on the front with some tight adjustable shocks, it would stay stiffer.<BR>Or if anyone has any suggestions on this subject, that would be great!<BR>BroCar 87||||reg||10
Z||000001||batboy||01-31-2001||09:54 PM||||Yeah, those posts would of been close to a year old. I have that problem much more under control now. Simple 5 step program.<P>1) Total suspension rebuild. Mods include lower front springs, stiffer rear coil-overs, poly bushings, and new everything else.<P>2) Moved the battery to the front. (Much, much better handling and road feel now, maybe not totally perfect.)<P>3) The next engine will have aluminum heads, so that should be 40-45 pounds less over the rear end.<P>4) I have a brand new front air dam I have yet to install.<P>5) I also have a pair of 86 Trans Am hood louvers I need to install.<P>Both of these two last items will eliminate air pooling up under the hood in the radiator compartment which possibly could lift the front end at speed. Of course, their are many variations to this plan that will work equally as well. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
